# Docs mission profile — sourced by tools/launchd/mission-control.sh when launchd
# sets MISSION_PROFILE=docs. Created 2026-08-28 (Mark, attended).
#
# THIS IS A VERSIONED COPY, NOT THE LIVE FILE. The driver sources
# ~/.config/ailang/mission-docs.env. Editing this file changes nothing on the rig —
# copy it across to deploy. (mission-env/README.md.)
#
# Convention (LOAD-BEARING, not style): every entry uses ${VAR:-value} so a
# command-line pin and pin-root.sh's exported MISSION_WORKDIR both still win. A bare
# assignment silently overrides its own caller — that is the bug that made the World
# loop's "driver pin:" line name the stale clone for weeks. The four identity vars
# below are the documented exception (the bootstrap guide's Step 3 shape).

MISSION_NAME=docs
MISSION_REPO=sunholo-data/ailang
MISSION_DOC=design_docs/docs-mission.md
# Its OWN clone. The website lives in this repo, which the V1 loop is also mutating
# every 90 minutes; two loops in one working tree is the hazard the concurrent-agent
# rule exists for. Separate clone = the motoko precedent.
MISSION_WORKDIR="${MISSION_WORKDIR:-/Users/voightkampff/dev/sunholo-data/ailang-docs}"

# --- PATH (the World iter-23 lesson, paid forward) ----------------------------
# The shared driver only PREPENDS $HOME/go/bin:$HOME/.local/bin to $PATH. A plist
# that carries no PATH leaves it homebrew-less, so `codex`, `gh`, `go` and `node`
# all resolve rc=127, every model pre-flight false-negatives, and every role
# silently degrades to opus — the exact opposite of this mission's purpose. The
# plist next door DOES set PATH; this line is the belt to that braces, because the
# failure is silent and reads like a quota outage.
PATH=/opt/homebrew/bin:$PATH

# =============================================================================
# ROUTING — cheap by construction (Mark, attended 2026-08-28)
# =============================================================================
# Mark's ask: "not need high powered models to run, but the best combination of
# cheaper models and smaller models from the subscriptions."
#
# This mission PROMOTES the flat-rate Ollama Cloud lanes from first-fallback to
# PRIMARY. That is a deliberate, scoped departure from the fleet policy ratified
# 2026-08-26 ("cloud sits at the FIRST FALLBACK... an unattended nightly loop is the
# wrong place to find that out"). The reasoning that policy rests on is what
# licenses the exception: the objection was the ABSENCE of role-specific evidence
# plus the COST of learning it somewhere consequential. A docs loop is the cheapest
# possible venue to generate exactly that evidence — a bad edit here is a typo on a
# web page, caught by the site build, not a miscompile. Every routing row this
# mission records is evidence the promotion rule can later act on for the fleet.
#
# SCOPE: this file only. v1/world/motoko are byte-for-byte unchanged, and
# test_mission_routing.sh asserts their primaries are still codex.

# Controller — the long orchestration session, and the single biggest line item
# (it re-reads a ~3.8k-line skill every fire). Opus-first is right for a loop that
# ships compiler changes; it is not right for one that fixes doc drift. Sonnet runs
# the gates. The driver's CONTROLLER_FALLBACK (codex:gpt-5.6-sol) still backs it, so
# the role is not single-provider.
MISSION_MODEL_PREFS="${MISSION_MODEL_PREFS:-claude-sonnet-5}"

# Designer — seed the rotation at the flat-rate lane, NOT the fable default. Fable is
# reserved for high-cognition spec synthesis; docs items are drift repair. The
# charter's Guardrails carry the matching rule, because the rotation itself lives in
# the shared skill and must not be forked for one mission.
MISSION_DESIGNER_MODEL="${MISSION_DESIGNER_MODEL:-pi:ollama/kimi-k3:cloud}"

# Planner — kimi-k3, flat-rate. Strongest open-weight model by external benchmark
# (88.3 Terminal-Bench 2.1), and planning is the role Mark already scoped it to
# ("the better models do planning only", 2026-08-26).
MISSION_PLANNER_MODEL="${MISSION_PLANNER_MODEL:-pi:ollama/kimi-k3:cloud}"
MISSION_PLANNER_FALLBACK="${MISSION_PLANNER_FALLBACK:-pi:openrouter/moonshotai/kimi-k3}"

# PLANNER ALLOWLIST — WITHOUT THIS LINE THE WHOLE ROUTING BLOCK ABOVE IS A NO-OP.
#
# derive-planner-lane.sh fails CLOSED to opus unless every path a design doc declares
# clears an allowlist that was, until 2026-08-28, infra-only (tools/launchd/*, two
# skill paths). Every docs design doc declares docs/... — so the pinned cheap planner
# would read as configured in the driver log while OPUS ran, every single iteration,
# on the one mission created specifically to avoid opus. Measured that day with a
# discriminating control: a docs/ doc emitted "opus fail-closed:path-not-in-codex-
# allowlist" while a byte-identical tools/launchd/ doc reached the pi lane.
#
# The gate is now per-mission data (default unchanged = infra-only). Widened HERE, and
# only here, to this mission's actual subject matter. It is still an ALLOWLIST:
# internal/**, cmd/**, and `docs/../internal/...` traversal are all still denied —
# all four properties are asserted in tools/launchd/test_mission_routing.sh.
MISSION_PLANNER_ALLOWLIST="${MISSION_PLANNER_ALLOWLIST:-tools/launchd/*|.claude/skills/mission-control/SKILL.md|.claude/skills/design-doc-creator/*|docs/*|examples/*|README.md|CHANGELOG.md}"

# Executor — the high-VOLUME role, so this is where flat-rate actually pays. Same
# deepseek-v4-flash weights the fleet already reaches as its first fallback, on the
# flat-rate route instead of metered OpenRouter: a route change, not a capability
# change. Degrades to the metered twin if the Ollama Cloud quota runs out (whose
# denominator is unpublished), so exhausting quota costs money, never capability.
MISSION_EXECUTOR_MODEL="${MISSION_EXECUTOR_MODEL:-pi:ollama/deepseek-v4-flash:0731-cloud}"
MISSION_EXECUTOR_FALLBACK="${MISSION_EXECUTOR_FALLBACK:-pi:openrouter/deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash-0731}"

# Evaluator — DELIBERATELY NOT DOWN-TIERED, and this is the one place this file
# spends real money on purpose.
#
# On a mission whose generator is deliberately cheap, the judge is the wrong place to
# economise: a cheap executor checked by a cheap judge means nobody catches anything,
# and the loop reports success it did not earn. Sonnet is cheap in absolute terms and
# is the proven mission evaluator. It also keeps generator!=judge structurally sound
# — Anthropic judging DeepSeek — where a minimax-on-cloud judge would share both the
# route and the quota pool with the executor, so one Ollama Cloud outage would take
# out generator and judge together.
MISSION_EVALUATOR_MODEL="${MISSION_EVALUATOR_MODEL:-sonnet}"
MISSION_EVALUATOR_FALLBACK="${MISSION_EVALUATOR_FALLBACK:-pi:ollama/minimax-m3:cloud,pi:openrouter/minimax/minimax-m3}"

# Metered ceiling — 1/5th of the fleet default ($5). Every primary lane here is
# flat-rate or subscription, so a fire that spends real dollars has already fallen
# off its intended route; a low ceiling turns that into a loud stop rather than a
# silent bill. Caps DOLLARS, not tokens.
MISSION_METERED_BUDGET_USD="${MISSION_METERED_BUDGET_USD:-1}"
